course: dictionary
lesson: 11185
title: Wisely and good judgement
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Wisely is an adverb. It tells you how someone does something: in a wise, sensible way, using good judgement. Many adverbs end in -ly, and that ending is a useful clue. «Earn, save, spend wisely» tells you to spend money in a sensible way, not in a rush.
explain: Wisely can also comment on a whole action. «Wisely, she wore a helmet.» Here the word sits at the start of the sentence and tells you that wearing a helmet was a good choice. It is not just describing how she put it on.
explain: Use the context to check your meaning. «A girl in Sydney gets $5 a week: she saves $2 in a jar and spends the rest wisely, and by the summer she can buy the kite she wanted.» She saves some, and she ends up with the kite, so spending wisely means spending in a way that works out well. Swap in sensibly and the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Wise means having good judgement, and wisely is the adverb form of wise.
fact: «Wisely» means in a wise, sensible way, using good judgement.
fact: Phrases often used with wisely include «choose wisely», «spend wisely» and «use your time wisely».
fact: In stories, wise characters such as an owl or a grandmother act or speak wisely, as in «She nodded wisely».
fact: A good check is to swap in sensibly or thoughtfully and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «With pocket money, save some and spend some wisely; money runs out if you spend it all at once.» What does spend wisely mean? | Give it all away today | *Spend in a sensible way, keeping some back | Spend all of it at once | Hide it under the bed
exercise: The owl nodded wisely. What does wisely suggest? | Very fast | Loudly and rudely | By accident | *In a thoughtful, sensible way, as a wise creature would
exercise: «Wisely, she wore a helmet.» What does wisely tell us about the helmet? | Wearing it was a silly choice | *Wearing it was a good, sensible choice | She lost the helmet | She wore it by mistake
test: Which phrase means use your time in a sensible way? | *Use your time wisely | Use your time wildly | Use your time quickly | Use your time sadly
test: Which means the same as choose wisely? | *Choose sensibly, using good judgement | Choose the first person you see | Choose at random, with your eyes closed | Choose in a hurry without thinking
exam: «For a child today: being right is not the same as speaking wisely.» What does speaking wisely mean? | *Speaking in a sensible, thoughtful way | Speaking in a secret code | Speaking loudly to win an argument | Speaking only when others are asleep
